{
  "id": "Compound::DB00517 - Gene::1129",
  "verbalization": "Anisotropine Methylbromide exhibits a strong binding interaction with Chrm1 and Chrm3, both of which are involved in G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity and G-protein coupled neurotransmitter receptor activity. These pathways are central to the regulation of cholinergic signaling, a key mechanism in neuromuscular and cardiac function. The shared involvement of Chrm2 in these receptor activities suggests a potential role for Anisotropine Methylbromide in modulating cholinergic signaling through the activation or inhibition of these receptors. The evidence from the three paths, which all point to Chrm2 being part of the same pathway as the target compound, supports a mechanistic link between Anisotropine Methylbromide and Chrm2, particularly through the modulation of cholinergic receptor activity. The consistency in the binding and pathway involvement across different paths strengthens the hypothesis that the compound exerts its effects via a cholinergic mechanism, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the exact mode of action and therapeutic relevance.",
